WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.869
Stage three, unit seven.
Small words, big work.

2
00:00:04.119 --> 00:00:07.529
This unit is about particles.
Each one is called a ḥarf.

3
00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:08.812
‫الحُرُوفُ‬

4
00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:12.110
A ḥarf never changes, and
it has no iʿrāb place.

5
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.473
Its job is to work on the words around it.

6
00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:17.985
Chapter one. Every ḥarf
is fixed, with no place.

7
00:00:18.335 --> 00:00:21.220
Pronouns, pointing words
and question isms are fixed.

8
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.047
But they still have a
place. Rafʿ, naṣb or jarr.

9
00:00:24.297 --> 00:00:28.115
A ḥarf is different. It is fixed,
and it has no place at all.

10
00:00:28.665 --> 00:00:30.604
Here is the phrase we use for a ḥarf.

11
00:00:30.854 --> 00:00:36.879
‫حَرْفٌ مَبْنِيٌّ لَا مَحَلَّ‬
‫لَهُ مِنَ الإِعْرَابِ‬

12
00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:39.383
A fixed ḥarf with no place in iʿrāb.

13
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:41.794
This unit has five groups of ḥarfs.

14
00:00:42.044 --> 00:00:44.699
First, the jarr ḥarfs,
such as from, to and in.

15
00:00:44.849 --> 00:00:47.107
The joining ḥarfs, like
wa, which means and.

16
00:00:47.257 --> 00:00:49.064
The answer ḥarfs, for yes and no.

17
00:00:49.214 --> 00:00:51.485
The question ḥarfs, for
a yes or no question.

18
00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:53.641
And the calling ḥarf, to call someone.

19
00:00:53.891 --> 00:00:55.480
Let us take them one by one.

20
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:59.574
Chapter two. The jarr ḥarfs.

21
00:00:59.724 --> 00:01:01.633
‫حُرُوفُ الجَرِّ‬

22
00:01:01.983 --> 00:01:04.696
You know four of them from
Stage one. Here are nine.

23
00:01:04.846 --> 00:01:06.537
The noun after each one is jarr.

24
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:07.807
‫مِنْ‬

25
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:08.754
From.

26
00:01:08.904 --> 00:01:10.798
‫خَرَجَ مِنَ البَيْتِ‬

27
00:01:11.048 --> 00:01:14.168
He went out of the house. The
house is jarr, with kasra.

28
00:01:14.518 --> 00:01:15.214
‫إِلَى‬

29
00:01:15.464 --> 00:01:16.067
To.

30
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:18.899
‫ذَهَبَ إِلَى المَسْجِدِ‬

31
00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:20.411
He went to the mosque.

32
00:01:20.761 --> 00:01:21.480
‫عَنْ‬

33
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.102
About, or away from.

34
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:25.528
‫الدَّرْسُ عَنِ القَمَرِ‬

35
00:01:25.778 --> 00:01:27.354
The lesson is about the moon.

36
00:01:27.704 --> 00:01:28.424
‫عَلَى‬

37
00:01:28.674 --> 00:01:29.298
On.

38
00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:31.955
‫الكِتَابُ عَلَى المَكْتَبِ‬

39
00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:33.518
The book is on the desk.

40
00:01:33.868 --> 00:01:34.578
‫فِي‬

41
00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:35.452
In.

42
00:01:35.602 --> 00:01:38.098
‫الطَّالِبُ فِي الفَصْلِ‬

43
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:40.173
The student is in the classroom.

44
00:01:40.523 --> 00:01:45.071
The sixth ḥarf means with, or by. It
is written joined to the word after it.

45
00:01:45.321 --> 00:01:47.306
‫كَتَبْتُ بِالقَلَمِ‬

46
00:01:47.556 --> 00:01:48.727
I wrote with the pen.

47
00:01:49.077 --> 00:01:52.500
The seventh means for, or
belongs to. It is joined too.

48
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.029
‫الكِتَابُ لِمُحَمَّدٍ‬

49
00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:56.878
The book belongs to Muhammad.

50
00:01:57.228 --> 00:01:59.531
The eighth means like. It is joined too.

51
00:01:59.781 --> 00:02:01.740
‫البِنْتُ كَالقَمَرِ‬

52
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.373
The girl is like the moon.

53
00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:04.536
‫حَتَّى‬

54
00:02:04.786 --> 00:02:05.525
Until.

55
00:02:05.675 --> 00:02:09.856
‫جَلَسْتُ فِي المَسْجِدِ حَتَّى الظُّهْرِ‬

56
00:02:10.106 --> 00:02:11.698
I sat in the mosque until noon.

57
00:02:12.048 --> 00:02:14.741
In every example, the noun
after the ḥarf is jarr.

58
00:02:15.291 --> 00:02:16.899
Two small changes before al.

59
00:02:17.149 --> 00:02:20.216
Look at the ḥarf that means
from. It ends in sukūn.

60
00:02:20.366 --> 00:02:21.086
‫مِنْ‬

61
00:02:21.336 --> 00:02:22.952
Before al, it takes a fatha.

62
00:02:23.102 --> 00:02:24.567
‫مِنَ البَيْتِ‬

63
00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:27.385
The ḥarf that means
about also ends in sukūn.

64
00:02:27.535 --> 00:02:28.254
‫عَنْ‬

65
00:02:28.504 --> 00:02:30.227
Before al, it takes a kasra.

66
00:02:30.377 --> 00:02:31.870
‫عَنِ القَمَرِ‬

67
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.747
This is only a helping vowel for easy
speaking. The ḥarf is still fixed.

68
00:02:37.297 --> 00:02:40.935
A tip. Three of these ḥarfs are
written joined to the next word.

69
00:02:41.085 --> 00:02:42.771
They mean with, for, and like.

70
00:02:43.021 --> 00:02:47.163
Look at the ḥarf that means for.
Before al, the alif of al drops out.

71
00:02:47.313 --> 00:02:48.836
‫لِلمُدَرِّسِ‬

72
00:02:49.086 --> 00:02:50.011
For the teacher.

73
00:02:52.366 --> 00:02:54.256
Chapter three. The joining ḥarfs.

74
00:02:54.406 --> 00:02:56.283
‫حُرُوفُ العَطْفِ‬

75
00:02:56.633 --> 00:02:58.561
They join a word to the word before it.

76
00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.415
The rule from Stage one stays.

77
00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:03.653
The word after them copies
the ending of the word before.

78
00:03:04.203 --> 00:03:08.373
The first is wa. It means and. It
is written joined to the next word.

79
00:03:08.523 --> 00:03:12.103
‫الكِتَابُ وَالقَلَمُ عَلَى المَكْتَبِ‬

80
00:03:12.353 --> 00:03:16.645
The book and the pen are on the desk.
The pen copies the damma of the book.

81
00:03:16.995 --> 00:03:20.441
The second ḥarf is also joined.
It means and, right after.

82
00:03:20.591 --> 00:03:22.998
‫دَخَلَ مُحَمَّدٌ فَخَالِدٌ‬

83
00:03:23.248 --> 00:03:25.618
Muhammad came in, and
Khalid right after him.

84
00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:26.861
‫ثُمَّ‬

85
00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:28.400
Then, after a while.

86
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.559
‫خَرَجَ حَامِدٌ ثُمَّ يَاسِرٌ‬

87
00:03:31.809 --> 00:03:33.423
Hamid went out, then Yasir.

88
00:03:33.773 --> 00:03:35.242
The fourth ḥarf means or.

89
00:03:35.392 --> 00:03:37.892
‫اِشْرَبِ المَاءَ أَوِ اللَّبَنَ‬

90
00:03:38.142 --> 00:03:39.703
Drink the water or the milk.

91
00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:40.770
‫بَلْ‬

92
00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.252
No, rather. It makes a correction.

93
00:03:43.402 --> 00:03:45.839
‫هَذَا كِتَابٌ بَلْ دَفْتَرٌ‬

94
00:03:46.089 --> 00:03:48.632
This is a book. No, rather a notebook.

95
00:03:49.182 --> 00:03:51.048
Let us look at two of them closely.

96
00:03:51.198 --> 00:03:53.605
‫دَخَلَ مُحَمَّدٌ فَخَالِدٌ‬

97
00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:56.225
Muhammad came in, and
Khalid right after him.

98
00:03:56.375 --> 00:03:58.479
Khalid copies the damma of Muhammad.

99
00:03:58.729 --> 00:03:59.805
‫مُحَمَّدٌ‬

100
00:04:00.055 --> 00:04:00.979
‫خَالِدٌ‬

101
00:04:01.429 --> 00:04:03.929
‫اِشْرَبِ المَاءَ أَوِ اللَّبَنَ‬

102
00:04:04.179 --> 00:04:05.740
Drink the water or the milk.

103
00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.898
The milk copies the fatha of the water.

104
00:04:08.148 --> 00:04:09.052
‫المَاءَ‬

105
00:04:09.302 --> 00:04:10.260
‫اللَّبَنَ‬

106
00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.553
Look at the ḥarf that
means or. It ends in sukūn.

107
00:04:13.703 --> 00:04:17.308
Before al, it takes a helping
kasra. The ḥarf is still fixed.

108
00:04:17.558 --> 00:04:18.567
‫أَوِ اللَّبَنَ‬

109
00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:22.824
Chapter four. The answer ḥarfs.

110
00:04:22.974 --> 00:04:24.856
‫حُرُوفُ الجَوَابِ‬

111
00:04:25.406 --> 00:04:26.223
‫نَعَمْ‬

112
00:04:26.473 --> 00:04:27.204
Yes.

113
00:04:27.354 --> 00:04:28.927
‫أَهَذَا بَيْتٌ؟‬

114
00:04:29.277 --> 00:04:30.389
Is this a house?

115
00:04:30.739 --> 00:04:32.528
‫نَعَمْ، هَذَا بَيْتٌ‬

116
00:04:32.778 --> 00:04:34.135
Yes, this is a house.

117
00:04:34.485 --> 00:04:35.224
‫لَا‬

118
00:04:35.474 --> 00:04:36.121
No.

119
00:04:36.271 --> 00:04:37.844
‫أَهَذَا بَيْتٌ؟‬

120
00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:39.865
‫لَا، هَذَا مَسْجِدٌ‬

121
00:04:40.115 --> 00:04:41.441
No, this is a mosque.

122
00:04:41.791 --> 00:04:42.512
‫بَلَى‬

123
00:04:42.762 --> 00:04:45.330
It means yes, to a question
with the word not in it.

124
00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.744
‫أَمَا ذَهَبْتَ إِلَى المَسْجِدِ؟‬

125
00:04:49.094 --> 00:04:50.437
Didn't you go to the mosque?

126
00:04:50.787 --> 00:04:52.346
‫بَلَى، ذَهَبْتُ‬

127
00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:53.537
Yes, I did.

128
00:04:54.087 --> 00:04:55.528
So when do we use this one?

129
00:04:55.878 --> 00:04:56.599
‫بَلَى‬

130
00:04:56.849 --> 00:05:00.691
Use it when the question has the word
not in it, and your answer is yes.

131
00:05:00.941 --> 00:05:02.517
Didn't you go? Yes, I did.

132
00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:05.111
A tip. Look at the question again.

133
00:05:05.261 --> 00:05:06.499
‫أَمَا ذَهَبْتَ؟‬

134
00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:08.423
Inside it is this ḥarf.

135
00:05:08.573 --> 00:05:09.288
‫مَا‬

136
00:05:09.538 --> 00:05:12.027
It means did not. You met it in unit six.

137
00:05:12.177 --> 00:05:13.420
‫مَا ذَهَبْتَ‬

138
00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:14.643
You did not go.

139
00:05:14.893 --> 00:05:17.050
Now add the joined question ḥarf in front.

140
00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:18.438
‫أَمَا ذَهَبْتَ؟‬

141
00:05:18.788 --> 00:05:20.864
Now it is a question. Didn't you go?

142
00:05:21.514 --> 00:05:23.548
Chapter five. The question ḥarfs.

143
00:05:23.698 --> 00:05:26.849
You met them in unit five.
They make a yes or no question.

144
00:05:26.999 --> 00:05:28.909
They are ḥarfs, so they have no place.

145
00:05:29.059 --> 00:05:31.574
The question words who, what
and where are different.

146
00:05:31.724 --> 00:05:34.050
They are isms, and they do have a place.

147
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:35.317
‫هَلْ‬

148
00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:37.191
It makes a yes or no question.

149
00:05:37.341 --> 00:05:41.361
‫هَلْ ذَهَبَ مُحَمَّدٌ إِلَى السُّوقِ؟‬

150
00:05:41.711 --> 00:05:43.241
Did Muhammad go to the market?

151
00:05:43.591 --> 00:05:47.731
The second one does the same job. It
is written joined to the next word.

152
00:05:47.881 --> 00:05:49.454
‫أَهَذَا بَيْتٌ؟‬

153
00:05:49.804 --> 00:05:50.916
Is this a house?

154
00:05:53.266 --> 00:05:54.981
Chapter six. The calling ḥarf.

155
00:05:55.131 --> 00:05:55.929
‫يَا‬

156
00:05:56.279 --> 00:05:57.654
Use it to call someone.

157
00:05:57.804 --> 00:06:02.071
After it, a name, or a single noun for
the person you call, takes one damma.

158
00:06:02.221 --> 00:06:03.657
It never takes tanween.

159
00:06:03.807 --> 00:06:05.146
This damma is fixed.

160
00:06:05.296 --> 00:06:07.360
The called word is in the place of naṣb.

161
00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.653
‫فِي مَحَلِّ نَصْبٍ‬

162
00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.606
Muhammad has tanween.

163
00:06:11.756 --> 00:06:12.832
‫مُحَمَّدٌ‬

164
00:06:13.082 --> 00:06:14.785
When we call him, one damma only.

165
00:06:14.935 --> 00:06:16.344
‫يَا مُحَمَّدُ‬

166
00:06:16.594 --> 00:06:17.509
O Muhammad.

167
00:06:17.859 --> 00:06:18.848
‫عَلِيٌّ‬

168
00:06:19.098 --> 00:06:20.458
‫يَا عَلِيُّ‬

169
00:06:20.708 --> 00:06:21.456
O Ali.

170
00:06:21.806 --> 00:06:22.885
‫آمِنَةُ‬

171
00:06:23.135 --> 00:06:25.875
Aminah already ends in one
damma, with no tanween.

172
00:06:26.025 --> 00:06:27.217
‫يَا آمِنَةُ‬

173
00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:28.386
O Aminah.

174
00:06:28.736 --> 00:06:31.185
A single noun for a
person works the same way.

175
00:06:31.335 --> 00:06:32.189
‫وَلَدٌ‬

176
00:06:32.439 --> 00:06:33.453
‫يَا وَلَدُ‬

177
00:06:33.703 --> 00:06:34.438
O boy.

178
00:06:34.788 --> 00:06:35.859
‫أُسْتَاذٌ‬

179
00:06:36.109 --> 00:06:37.532
‫يَا أُسْتَاذُ‬

180
00:06:37.782 --> 00:06:38.668
O teacher.

181
00:06:38.918 --> 00:06:41.642
Each called word ends in
one damma, with no tanween.

182
00:06:42.192 --> 00:06:45.709
A tip. This is the same as
lesson five of the Madinah book.

183
00:06:45.959 --> 00:06:47.190
‫يَا بِلَالُ‬

184
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.467
‫يَا شَيْخُ‬

185
00:06:48.717 --> 00:06:50.232
O Bilal. O old man.

186
00:06:50.882 --> 00:06:54.068
Chapter seven. A ḥarf
or an ism? Look-alikes.

187
00:06:54.218 --> 00:06:58.457
Some ḥarfs look like isms. And
some short question words are isms.

188
00:06:58.607 --> 00:07:00.111
Read the vowels carefully.

189
00:07:00.661 --> 00:07:01.381
‫مِنْ‬

190
00:07:01.631 --> 00:07:02.589
From. A ḥarf.

191
00:07:02.739 --> 00:07:03.460
‫مَنْ‬

192
00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.855
Who. An ism. Only the
first vowel is different.

193
00:07:07.205 --> 00:07:07.925
‫عَلَى‬

194
00:07:08.175 --> 00:07:09.087
On. A ḥarf.

195
00:07:09.237 --> 00:07:10.226
‫عَلِيٌّ‬

196
00:07:10.476 --> 00:07:11.949
Ali, a name. An ism.

197
00:07:12.299 --> 00:07:13.016
‫هَلْ‬

198
00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.760
It starts a yes or no question. A ḥarf.

199
00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:16.758
‫أَيْنَ‬

200
00:07:17.008 --> 00:07:21.212
Where. An ism. Both are used in
questions. Only one is a ḥarf.

201
00:07:21.562 --> 00:07:25.645
Next, the ḥarf that means like. It
is written joined to the next word.

202
00:07:25.795 --> 00:07:26.512
‫كَمْ‬

203
00:07:26.762 --> 00:07:30.206
How many. An ism. Both
start with the same letter.

204
00:07:30.756 --> 00:07:31.608
In short.

205
00:07:31.758 --> 00:07:33.803
Every ḥarf is fixed and has no place.

206
00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:37.381
‫لَا مَحَلَّ لَهُ مِنَ الإِعْرَابِ‬

207
00:07:37.731 --> 00:07:39.933
After a jarr ḥarf, the next noun is jarr.

208
00:07:40.183 --> 00:07:44.095
After a joining ḥarf, the next word
copies the ending of the word before.

209
00:07:44.345 --> 00:07:46.110
The answer ḥarfs say yes and no.

210
00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:46.981
‫بَلَى‬

211
00:07:47.231 --> 00:07:49.974
This one says yes to a question
with the word not in it.

212
00:07:50.224 --> 00:07:52.305
The question ḥarfs make
a yes or no question.

213
00:07:52.455 --> 00:07:54.934
After the calling ḥarf,
a name takes one damma.

214
00:07:55.084 --> 00:07:56.493
‫يَا مُحَمَّدُ‬

215
00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:59.577
Now it is your turn.
Open the Practice tab.
